   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 929 of the laws of 1986 amending the tax law and other\nlaws relating to the metropolitan transportation authority, in relation to\nextending certain provisions thereof applicable to the resolution of labor\ndisputes",
    "basePrintNo": "S6737",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6737-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §45, Chap 929 of 1986",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6737\n\nSPONSOR: GOUNARDES\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 929 of the laws of 1986 amending the tax law and\nother laws relating to the metropolitan transportation authority, in\nrelation to extending certain provisions thereof applicable to the\nresolution of labor disputes\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would continue certain provisions related to the resolution of\nlabor disputes during collective bargaining negotiations between the\nMetropolitan Transportation Authority (MTA) and its labor represen-\ntatives in order to promote settlements and avoid disruptions in transit\nservice.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 would amend section 45 of Chapter 929 of the Laws of 1986 to\nextend to July 1, 2023 the enforcement of certain provisions under the\nTax Law and Public Authorities Law which provide for the oversight of\nbinding arbitration by the Public Employment Relations Board (PERB)\nbetween the Metropolitan Transportation Authority (MTA) and its labor\nrepresentatives in order to promote settlements and avoid disruptions in\ntransit service.\n\nSection 2 would make the bill effective immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCompulsory arbitration has promoted the resolution of labor management\ndisputes in furtherance of uninterrupted service on the MTA transit\nsystem and its bridges and tunnels. Evaluations of the effects of this\nlaw have historically been positive. This will extend this provision for\nan additional two years.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019: Chapter 55/ TED, Part HH 2017:  Chapter 63\n\n2015: Chapter 28\n\n2013: Chapter 72 2011: Chapter 105\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTBD\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediate\n",
